1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,799
If you connect the two ends
of a resistor with a wire,

2
00:00:02,879 --> 00:00:05,610
how much current flows
through that resistor?

3
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:07,906
The first thought that comes
to mind might be this:

4
00:00:07,986 --> 00:00:11,410
in a short circuit the current is zero;
no current flows through the wire.

5
00:00:11,490 --> 00:00:15,727
Yet in the circuit we're about to build,
exactly 3 mA flows through the wire,

6
00:00:15,807 --> 00:00:18,940
while no current at all
flows through the resistor.

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,050
In a short circuit, what's zero
is not the current, but the voltage.

8
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:24,282
Confusing an open circuit
with a short circuit

9
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:26,620
is the most common mistake on this topic.

10
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:27,900
By the end of this part,

11
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:32,010
you'll calculate voltage from energy
and write every voltage with its sign.

12
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:35,320
You'll know what a point's
voltage is measured relative to.

13
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,720
You'll find the current in a short circuit

14
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,640
and see what an ideal
voltage source guarantees.

15
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:45,990
And in a datasheet, you'll learn
where a real source stops being ideal.

16
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:59,640
Hello everyone, welcome
to the Circuit Theory I series.

17
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:03,215
Today, in the third part of Lecture 1,
we'll talk about voltage,

18
00:01:03,295 --> 00:01:06,280
the short circuit
and the ideal voltage source.

19
00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:10,574
In Part 2, we saw that current
has a value and a direction,

20
00:01:10,654 --> 00:01:14,960
and that an open circuit guarantees
only that the current is zero.

21
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:19,430
Now we move on to the second fundamental
quantity of circuit theory: voltage.

22
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:23,373
In physics, this quantity is also
called potential difference.

23
00:01:23,453 --> 00:01:27,173
In circuit theory, we'll call it
voltage or voltage difference.

24
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:28,343
Its unit is the volt.

25
00:01:28,483 --> 00:01:30,303
So what exactly is its definition?

26
00:01:30,383 --> 00:01:32,443
Let's pick two points in a large circuit.

27
00:01:32,523 --> 00:01:34,733
Let's call one of them a and the other b.

28
00:01:34,813 --> 00:01:37,964
We define the voltage
between a and b like this:

29
00:01:38,044 --> 00:01:40,931
the energy we must spend
to carry a positive

30
00:01:41,011 --> 00:01:44,303
charge of one coulomb
from point b to point a.

31
00:01:44,383 --> 00:01:48,203
Pay attention to the order:
the charge starts at b and goes to a.

32
00:01:48,283 --> 00:01:50,893
This order will determine
the sign of the voltage.

33
00:01:50,973 --> 00:01:53,383
We don't have to work
with a charge of one coulomb.

34
00:01:53,463 --> 00:01:57,723
We can carry any positive charge and
divide the energy spent by that charge.

35
00:01:57,803 --> 00:02:00,173
So voltage is energy divided by charge.

36
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:03,083
The unit of energy is the joule,
and the unit of charge is the coulomb.

37
00:02:03,163 --> 00:02:05,753
1 J / 1 C = 1 V.

38
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:08,414
For example,
if a battery is labeled 1.5 V,

39
00:02:08,494 --> 00:02:11,558
carrying a positive charge
of 1 C from its negative

40
00:02:11,638 --> 00:02:15,283
terminal to its positive
terminal takes 1.5 J of energy.

41
00:02:15,363 --> 00:02:16,363
Let's do an example.

42
00:02:16,443 --> 00:02:22,293
Carrying a positive charge of 4 µC
from b to a takes 18 µJ of energy.

43
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:25,523
The voltage between a and b
is energy divided by charge.

44
00:02:25,603 --> 00:02:28,193
18 µJ / 4 µC.

45
00:02:28,273 --> 00:02:33,153
The micros cancel, leaving 18 / 4 = 4.5 V.

46
00:02:33,233 --> 00:02:36,453
Now let's double the charge: 8 µC.

47
00:02:36,533 --> 00:02:39,153
This time, carrying it takes 36 µJ.

48
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:42,413
36 / 8, again 4.5 V.

49
00:02:42,493 --> 00:02:46,883
The charge doubled, the energy doubled
too, and the voltage didn't change.

50
00:02:46,963 --> 00:02:49,885
Because voltage depends not
on the amount of charge we carry,

51
00:02:49,965 --> 00:02:51,793
but on the two points themselves.

52
00:02:51,873 --> 00:02:55,723
And what if we take the same
4 µC from a to b this time?

53
00:02:55,803 --> 00:02:58,643
Then we don't spend this
energy; we get it back.

54
00:02:58,723 --> 00:03:01,663
The energy spent becomes −18 µJ.

55
00:03:01,743 --> 00:03:05,153
−18 / 4 = −4.5 V.

56
00:03:05,233 --> 00:03:09,533
So the voltage between b and a is −4.5 V.

57
00:03:09,643 --> 00:03:12,723
You can subscribe to the channel
to follow the rest of this series.

58
00:03:14,483 --> 00:03:17,463
There's an easy way
to picture this definition.

59
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:20,593
You can think of voltage
as a difference in height.

60
00:03:20,673 --> 00:03:24,873
For a positive charge, the plus end is
a hilltop and the minus end is a valley.

61
00:03:24,953 --> 00:03:27,953
Lifting the charge from the valley
to the hilltop takes energy.

62
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:31,363
And coming down from the hilltop
to the valley, you get that energy back.

63
00:03:31,443 --> 00:03:35,303
This energy per coulomb
is the voltage between the two points.

64
00:03:35,383 --> 00:03:39,563
This is only an analogy, but it's
very useful for remembering the sign.

65
00:03:39,693 --> 00:03:42,983
In Part 2, current had
a value and a direction.

66
00:03:43,063 --> 00:03:45,713
Voltage also has a value and a sign.

67
00:03:45,793 --> 00:03:50,463
If we say there are 7 V across a resistor,
saying that alone isn't enough.

68
00:03:50,543 --> 00:03:53,753
We put a plus on one end
and a minus on the other.

69
00:03:53,833 --> 00:03:57,923
This means the plus end is 7 V
higher than the minus end.

70
00:03:58,003 --> 00:04:00,193
What happens if we put
the signs the other way around?

71
00:04:00,273 --> 00:04:01,803
Same resistor, same situation.

72
00:04:01,883 --> 00:04:04,123
But this time we put
the plus on the other end.

73
00:04:04,203 --> 00:04:07,523
Then the number we have to write is −7 V.

74
00:04:07,603 --> 00:04:10,967
In short, the voltage
between b and a is the negative

75
00:04:11,047 --> 00:04:13,283
of the voltage between a and b.

76
00:04:13,363 --> 00:04:15,213
You can also read this
the other way around.

77
00:04:15,293 --> 00:04:18,019
We put a plus on the left
end of a resistor and a minus

78
00:04:18,099 --> 00:04:20,823
on the right end,
and the calculation gave −3 V.

79
00:04:20,903 --> 00:04:23,483
That doesn't mean
the calculation is wrong.

80
00:04:23,563 --> 00:04:27,513
It just tells us the right end
is 3 V higher than the left end.

81
00:04:27,593 --> 00:04:30,332
You don't have to guess the signs
correctly from the start;

82
00:04:30,412 --> 00:04:33,383
the result, together with its
sign, tells you the truth.

83
00:04:33,463 --> 00:04:34,543
Let's go back to our example.

84
00:04:34,623 --> 00:04:37,683
There were 4.5 V between a and b.

85
00:04:37,763 --> 00:04:40,503
We put the plus on a and the minus on b.

86
00:04:40,583 --> 00:04:44,183
So point a is 4.5 V higher than point b.

87
00:04:44,263 --> 00:04:47,563
As long as the signs are
drawn on the schematic.

88
00:04:47,643 --> 00:04:50,378
A voltage without signs
is incomplete information,

89
00:04:50,458 --> 00:04:52,623
like a current without an arrow.

90
00:04:53,053 --> 00:04:55,300
Now we come to a very important point:

91
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:58,463
voltage is meaningful
only between two points.

92
00:04:58,543 --> 00:05:01,528
If a friend takes a measurement
and says there are 12 V at this point,

93
00:05:01,608 --> 00:05:04,123
the first thing you should
ask is: relative to what?

94
00:05:04,203 --> 00:05:08,983
12 V is the difference
between that point and some other point.

95
00:05:09,063 --> 00:05:12,633
If that other point isn't specified,
the number you're given is incomplete.

96
00:05:12,713 --> 00:05:16,463
In practice, that other point
is usually the circuit's ground.

97
00:05:16,543 --> 00:05:20,193
Ground is a common terminal we choose
as the reference in the circuit.

98
00:05:20,273 --> 00:05:22,333
On a schematic,
it's shown with this symbol.

99
00:05:22,413 --> 00:05:24,534
When we talk about the voltage of a point,

100
00:05:24,614 --> 00:05:27,463
we mean the voltage
between that point and ground.

101
00:05:27,543 --> 00:05:28,863
Why do we do this?

102
00:05:28,943 --> 00:05:31,283
A circuit can have dozens of points.

103
00:05:31,363 --> 00:05:34,300
Instead of stating separately
what each one is relative to,

104
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:37,033
we give them all relative
to the same terminal.

105
00:05:37,113 --> 00:05:39,797
That way, by subtracting
the voltages of two points,

106
00:05:39,877 --> 00:05:42,623
we immediately find
the voltage between them.

107
00:05:42,743 --> 00:05:48,403
Let's do an example. Relative to ground,
point a is at 5 V and point b is at 3.3 V.

108
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:52,943
The voltage between a and b is the voltage
of a minus the voltage of b.

109
00:05:53,023 --> 00:05:56,353
5 − 3.3 = 1.7 V.

110
00:05:56,433 --> 00:06:01,073
Conversely, the voltage
between b and a is −1.7 V.

111
00:06:01,163 --> 00:06:02,783
Now let's change the reference.

112
00:06:02,863 --> 00:06:04,703
Let's move ground to point b.

113
00:06:04,783 --> 00:06:07,043
The voltage of b is now 0 V.

114
00:06:07,123 --> 00:06:11,663
And the voltage of a is 5 − 3.3 = 1.7 V.

115
00:06:11,743 --> 00:06:17,143
And the voltage of the old
ground is now 0 − 3.3 = −3.3 V.

116
00:06:17,223 --> 00:06:20,583
The voltages of the points changed,
because the reference changed.

117
00:06:20,663 --> 00:06:22,883
And the voltage between a and b?

118
00:06:22,963 --> 00:06:26,763
1.7 − 0, again 1.7 V.

119
00:06:26,843 --> 00:06:30,133
The voltage between two points
doesn't depend on the reference.

120
00:06:30,213 --> 00:06:32,893
Keep this number, 1.7 V, in mind.

121
00:06:32,973 --> 00:06:34,985
Soon we'll come across the same number

122
00:06:35,065 --> 00:06:38,093
between the input and output
of a real regulator.

123
00:06:38,617 --> 00:06:40,107
Next up: the short circuit.

124
00:06:40,187 --> 00:06:42,373
A short circuit means
connecting two points

125
00:06:42,453 --> 00:06:45,367
with a wire whose resistance
is taken to be zero.

126
00:06:45,447 --> 00:06:47,717
A short circuit guarantees
us only one thing:

127
00:06:47,797 --> 00:06:50,947
the voltage between the two
points it connects is zero.

128
00:06:51,027 --> 00:06:52,917
We can see this from Ohm's law.

129
00:06:52,997 --> 00:06:54,957
Voltage is resistance times current.

130
00:06:55,037 --> 00:06:56,387
The wire's resistance is zero.

131
00:06:56,467 --> 00:07:01,127
As long as the current is finite,
zero times the current is still zero.

132
00:07:01,207 --> 00:07:06,397
For example, even if 10 mA flows
through the wire, 0 Ω × 10 mA = 0 V.

133
00:07:06,477 --> 00:07:09,387
Can we say the same
thing about current? No.

134
00:07:09,467 --> 00:07:11,490
The wire only forces the voltage to zero;

135
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:15,577
how much current flows through it
is decided by the rest of the circuit.

136
00:07:15,657 --> 00:07:17,427
Let's put this side by side with Part 2.

137
00:07:17,507 --> 00:07:19,684
An open circuit guarantees
that the current is zero;

138
00:07:19,764 --> 00:07:22,147
the rest of the circuit
determines the voltage.

139
00:07:22,227 --> 00:07:24,357
A short circuit guarantees
that the voltage is zero;

140
00:07:24,437 --> 00:07:26,797
the rest of the circuit
determines the current.

141
00:07:27,007 --> 00:07:29,787
Now let's build the circuit
from the beginning of the lecture.

142
00:07:29,867 --> 00:07:33,495
A 6 V voltage source and two
resistors, 2 kΩ and 1 kΩ,

143
00:07:33,575 --> 00:07:36,897
are connected in series
to form a single loop.

144
00:07:36,977 --> 00:07:41,303
The same current flows through two
resistors in series and their voltages add

145
00:07:41,383 --> 00:07:45,757
up, so together they present a resistance
of 2 + 1 = 3 kΩ to the current.

146
00:07:45,837 --> 00:07:47,667
We'll look at this in detail later.

147
00:07:47,747 --> 00:07:51,797
Without the wire,
the current is 6 V / 3 kΩ = 2 mA.

148
00:07:51,877 --> 00:07:56,197
Now we connect the two ends
of the 1 kΩ resistor with a wire.

149
00:07:56,317 --> 00:07:58,457
This is where the most
common mistake is made.

150
00:07:58,537 --> 00:08:00,358
Saying the current
in a short circuit is zero,

151
00:08:00,438 --> 00:08:02,461
and assuming that no current
flows through the wire

152
00:08:02,541 --> 00:08:05,107
and that the current still
flows through both resistors.

153
00:08:05,187 --> 00:08:10,127
With this mistake, the current again comes
out as 2 mA. The correct way is this.

154
00:08:10,207 --> 00:08:14,457
The wire forces the voltage
across the 1 kΩ resistor to zero.

155
00:08:14,537 --> 00:08:19,607
By Ohm's law, this resistor's
current is 0 V / 1 kΩ, that is, zero.

156
00:08:19,687 --> 00:08:22,307
The current bypasses the resistor
and flows through the wire.

157
00:08:22,387 --> 00:08:26,577
Now the 2 kΩ resistor sees
the source's 6 V on its own.

158
00:08:26,657 --> 00:08:29,957
The current is 6 V / 2 kΩ = 3 mA.

159
00:08:30,037 --> 00:08:32,667
This 3 mA flows through the wire.

160
00:08:32,747 --> 00:08:36,607
The shorted resistor now behaves as if
it weren't in the circuit at all.

161
00:08:36,687 --> 00:08:39,887
In a short circuit, what's zero
is the voltage, not the current.

162
00:08:39,967 --> 00:08:42,367
In this example,
we assumed the source was ideal.

163
00:08:42,447 --> 00:08:45,286
An ideal voltage source
is an element that keeps the voltage

164
00:08:45,366 --> 00:08:48,787
across its terminals the same,
no matter what is connected to it.

165
00:08:48,867 --> 00:08:52,047
The current, however, is determined not
by the source but by the connected load.

166
00:08:52,127 --> 00:08:53,377
If a single resistor is connected,

167
00:08:53,457 --> 00:08:55,917
the current is the source voltage
divided by the resistance.

168
00:08:55,997 --> 00:08:57,357
This model has a limit.

169
00:08:57,437 --> 00:09:00,474
If we short the terminals of an ideal
source directly with a wire,

170
00:09:00,554 --> 00:09:02,167
we run into a contradiction.

171
00:09:02,247 --> 00:09:05,097
The source says: there are
6 V across my terminals.

172
00:09:05,177 --> 00:09:06,707
The wire says: 0 V.

173
00:09:06,787 --> 00:09:10,587
To see this, let's shrink the resistor
connected to the source step by step.

174
00:09:10,667 --> 00:09:13,727
At 1 Ω, the current is 6 / 1 = 6 A.

175
00:09:13,807 --> 00:09:18,147
At 0.1 Ω, 6 / 0.1 = 60 A.

176
00:09:18,227 --> 00:09:21,537
At 0.01 Ω, 600 A.

177
00:09:21,617 --> 00:09:24,997
As the resistance approaches zero,
the current grows without limit.

178
00:09:25,077 --> 00:09:28,917
So the ideal source model loses
its meaning in a short circuit.

179
00:09:28,997 --> 00:09:33,247
Next, we'll see in a datasheet
what happens in a real source.

180
00:09:33,717 --> 00:09:36,507
Let's look at the voltage
rows in a datasheet.

181
00:09:36,587 --> 00:09:39,487
There, every voltage is given
between two terminals.

182
00:09:39,567 --> 00:09:42,014
In most rows, the name tells
you those two terminals;

183
00:09:42,094 --> 00:09:44,787
if it doesn't, the voltage
is relative to ground.

184
00:09:44,947 --> 00:09:49,427
The datasheet of the electronic switch
from Part 2 has two voltage limits.

185
00:09:49,507 --> 00:09:53,227
The first is 60 V between the two
terminals the current flows through.

186
00:09:53,307 --> 00:09:57,705
The second is between the control
terminal and the switch's lower terminal:

187
00:09:57,785 --> 00:10:01,837
the control terminal can be at most
20 V above or 20 V below it.

188
00:10:01,917 --> 00:10:05,051
If you apply 24 V between the control
terminal and the lower terminal

189
00:10:05,131 --> 00:10:08,717
because the part is rated for 60 V,
you can damage the control terminal.

190
00:10:08,797 --> 00:10:11,901
The diode also has two separate
rows for the same two terminals:

191
00:10:11,981 --> 00:10:14,694
in the forward direction,
at most 1.1 V drops across it;

192
00:10:14,774 --> 00:10:17,417
in the reverse direction,
it withstands 1000 V.

193
00:10:17,497 --> 00:10:21,047
Same two points, opposite sign,
a completely different value.

194
00:10:21,127 --> 00:10:24,407
So when you read a voltage row,
ask two questions.

195
00:10:24,487 --> 00:10:26,747
Between which two
terminals is this voltage?

196
00:10:26,827 --> 00:10:29,927
With which sign, that is,
which terminal is higher?

197
00:10:30,007 --> 00:10:35,247
Then, as in Part 2, check the conditions
too: at what temperature, at what current?

198
00:10:35,327 --> 00:10:40,267
Now let's look at a real voltage
source: a 3.3 V regulator.

199
00:10:40,347 --> 00:10:44,187
A regulator is an integrated circuit
that keeps its output voltage constant.

200
00:10:44,267 --> 00:10:47,657
We feed its input 5 V
from a phone charger.

201
00:10:47,767 --> 00:10:48,887
Is the input enough?

202
00:10:48,967 --> 00:10:50,142
In the worst case,

203
00:10:50,222 --> 00:10:55,037
this regulator's input must be
at least 238 mV higher than its output.

204
00:10:55,117 --> 00:10:59,647
238 mV, that is, 0.238 V.

205
00:10:59,727 --> 00:11:05,507
3.3 + 0.238 = 3.538 V.

206
00:11:05,587 --> 00:11:07,113
5 V is greater than that,

207
00:11:07,193 --> 00:11:11,047
and it's also below the recommended
maximum input of 5.5 V.

208
00:11:11,127 --> 00:11:16,307
The voltage between input
and output is 5 − 3.3 = 1.7 V.

209
00:11:16,387 --> 00:11:18,197
The very same number as before.

210
00:11:18,277 --> 00:11:20,387
Let's connect three loads
to the output, one at a time.

211
00:11:20,467 --> 00:11:25,787
330 Ω: the current
is 3.3 V / 330 Ω = 10 mA.

212
00:11:25,867 --> 00:11:30,157
66 Ω: 3.3 / 66 = 50 mA.

213
00:11:30,237 --> 00:11:34,077
33 Ω: 3.3 / 33 = 100 mA.

214
00:11:34,157 --> 00:11:39,017
In every case, there are 3.3 V across
the load; only the current changes.

215
00:11:39,097 --> 00:11:42,177
This is exactly how an ideal
voltage source behaves.

216
00:11:42,257 --> 00:11:43,397
How good is the model?

217
00:11:43,477 --> 00:11:45,011
According to the datasheet,

218
00:11:45,091 --> 00:11:48,847
the output changes by about 60
mV for every ampere of current.

219
00:11:48,927 --> 00:11:53,107
Going from 10 mA to 100 mA,
the difference is 90 mA.

220
00:11:53,187 --> 00:11:57,077
90 mA, that is, 0.090 A.

221
00:11:57,157 --> 00:12:00,977
0.060 × 0.090 = 0.0054 V,

222
00:12:01,057 --> 00:12:03,707
that is, 5.4 mV.

223
00:12:03,787 --> 00:12:07,240
Even over the entire recommended range,

224
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:11,227
the change is 60
mV/A × about 0.5 A ≈ 30 mV.

225
00:12:11,307 --> 00:12:14,717
But this range goes up to 500 mA.

226
00:12:14,797 --> 00:12:16,553
In terms of current,

227
00:12:16,633 --> 00:12:22,607
the smallest resistance you can
connect is 3.3 V / 0.5 A = 6.6 Ω.

228
00:12:22,687 --> 00:12:28,417
But in this setup, that much current heats
the regulator enough to shut it down.

229
00:12:28,497 --> 00:12:31,182
We'll calculate the heating
when we cover power;

230
00:12:31,262 --> 00:12:34,427
that's why we used at most
100 mA in our example.

231
00:12:34,547 --> 00:12:37,547
And what if we connect
3.3 Ω to the output?

232
00:12:37,627 --> 00:12:41,937
The ideal model says 3.3 / 3.3 = 1 A.

233
00:12:42,017 --> 00:12:46,447
But this regulator's current
limit is at most 865 mA.

234
00:12:46,527 --> 00:12:50,517
The regulator limits the current,
and the output can't stay at 3.3 V.

235
00:12:50,597 --> 00:12:53,227
It no longer behaves like an ideal source.

236
00:12:53,307 --> 00:12:55,817
What if we short the output with a wire?

237
00:12:55,897 --> 00:13:00,027
The voltage across the wire is zero;
that's what a short circuit guarantees.

238
00:13:00,107 --> 00:13:01,907
The current, on the other hand,
is set by the rest of the circuit.

239
00:13:01,987 --> 00:13:04,937
Here, the rest of the circuit means
the regulator's own current limit.

240
00:13:05,017 --> 00:13:06,476
When the output drops too low,

241
00:13:06,556 --> 00:13:09,506
the regulator cuts the current
back even further on its own;

242
00:13:09,586 --> 00:13:12,797
in a short circuit, the current
typically falls to 355 mA.

243
00:13:12,877 --> 00:13:15,449
The regulator heats up,
shuts down when it overheats,

244
00:13:15,529 --> 00:13:17,747
and turns back on when it cools down.

245
00:13:17,827 --> 00:13:20,414
Another costly mistake in design is this:

246
00:13:20,494 --> 00:13:23,082
assuming that a source labeled 3.3 V will

247
00:13:23,162 --> 00:13:26,527
deliver 3.3 V no matter
what you connect to it.

248
00:13:26,607 --> 00:13:29,849
A real source keeps its
ideal behavior only within

249
00:13:29,929 --> 00:13:33,457
the current and heating
limits in its datasheet.

250
00:13:33,977 --> 00:13:34,977
Let's sum up.

251
00:13:35,057 --> 00:13:39,433
Voltage is the energy needed to carry
a unit positive charge from one point

252
00:13:39,513 --> 00:13:43,997
to another, and it is always written
between two points, with its sign.

253
00:13:44,077 --> 00:13:48,427
A point's voltage is relative
to a reference, usually ground.

254
00:13:48,507 --> 00:13:51,056
An open circuit guarantees
only that the current is zero,

255
00:13:51,136 --> 00:13:53,368
and a short circuit only
that the voltage is zero;

256
00:13:53,448 --> 00:13:56,267
the rest of the circuit
determines the other quantity.

257
00:13:56,347 --> 00:13:58,796
An ideal voltage source
holds the voltage constant,

258
00:13:58,876 --> 00:14:00,987
and the load determines the current.

259
00:14:01,067 --> 00:14:03,895
A real source does this
only within the current

260
00:14:03,975 --> 00:14:06,557
and heating limits in its datasheet.

261
00:14:06,847 --> 00:14:08,237
Let me leave you with a question too.

262
00:14:08,317 --> 00:14:13,287
We connect a 22 Ω resistor
to the output of a 3.3 V regulator.

263
00:14:13,367 --> 00:14:16,507
The regulator's maximum
output current is 500 mA.

264
00:14:16,587 --> 00:14:20,357
What will the voltage across the resistor
and the current through it be?

265
00:14:20,437 --> 00:14:21,957
Write your answer in the comments.

266
00:14:22,197 --> 00:14:23,463
If this lecture helped you,

267
00:14:23,543 --> 00:14:26,497
please like the video
and share it with your classmates.

268
00:14:26,577 --> 00:14:29,397
If you subscribe to the channel,
you won't miss Lecture 2.

269
00:14:29,477 --> 00:14:33,063
In the first part of Lecture 2,
we'll look at the ideal wire,

270
00:14:33,143 --> 00:14:36,677
the ideal switch, and why
a real battery isn't ideal.

